// bubbler profile: libreoffice
//
// `etc-share "libreoffice"`: this installation's `bootstraprc`,
// `sofficerc` and the printer setup `share/psprint/psprint.conf` are
// symlinks from the install tree into
// /etc/libreoffice, and the baseline hides everything in /etc that is not
// on its allowlist. Without `bootstraprc` the suite has no
// `UserInstallation` path, so soffice.bin exits 1 before it writes its
// user profile and no window ever maps (measured). An installation that
// keeps those files in the program directory itself wants the line
// removed rather than kept: a share whose source is missing is an error.
//
// The splash screen prints `Failed to open display` on the way past: it
// draws itself with Xlib, finds no X server, and gives up on the splash
// alone. Nothing else in the suite needs one.
//
// No `env` node names a VCL plugin. bubbler clears the environment, and
// with nothing to autodetect from LibreOffice picks the gtk3 plugin by
// itself — with no such node, `libvclplug_gtk3lo.so` is what soffice.bin
// maps; `env SAL_USE_VCLPLUGIN="qt6"` picks another.
//
// Documents live in ~/Documents and in the private home. A directory on
// another disk is named explicitly, e.g.
//     path-share "/kioxia/docs" mode=rw
//
//
// `userns "disable"`: LibreOffice nests no sandbox of its own, so the door a
// nested user namespace opens stays shut.
// Bare by design. Add:
//   dri
//     — the GPU. Without it LibreOffice draws in software, which is what
//       it did by default for most of its life.
//   dbus
//   portals
//     — the file chooser that reaches outside the shared directories: the
//       portal runs it on the host and exports what you pick into this
//       instance's document view, and the path it hands back opens there.
//       Without them the chooser is the one inside the sandbox and sees
//       the private home and ~/Documents.
wayland
home-share "Documents" mode=rw
etc-share "libreoffice"
// The start centre, not one of the seven per-document entries that run
// the same command with a `--writer`-style argument.
userns "disable"
desktop "libreoffice-startcenter.desktop"
command "libreoffice"
